{"text": "Brief History of Nuclear Science and Engineering Institute, NSEI\n(Graduate Program in Nuclear Engineering, Medical Physics, Health Physics, Since 1964)\n\n1955: First Nuclear Engineering Classes taught\n1964: Nuclear Engineering Program (NEP) established under Graduate School; First Nuclear\nEngineering MS awarded\n1977: Medical Physics Program established\n1990: Nuclear Engineering Degree moved to College of Engineering from Graduate School\n2001-02: Engineering Dean plans to dismantle Nuclear Engineering Program (NEP) in the\nCollege of Engineering, and disburse the faculty.\n2002-03: Chancellor Richard Wallace transfers NEP (together with five tenured faculty, staff,\nspace, degrees, budget) from College of Engineering to the Graduate School, creating NSEI.\nInterim director appointed. No new faculty positions created. The five tenured positions were\n(are) not full fully funded, following a 8.5% cut during budget transfer.\n2003: NSEI awarded a $3.0M/5 year INIE Mid-West Consortium Award (only one of the five in\nthe nation) by the Department of Energy.\n2006: Interim Director retires. First search for permanent Director is unsuccessful. No new\nsearch is started, and no interim director has been appointed since.\n2007: NSEI is awarded 3.0 M/3-5 year VHTR Consortium Award (as the lead University) by DOE,\nwinning against top institutions. Also, NSEI is placed first in the nation for Faculty Scholarly\nProductivity by Academic Analytics.\n2008-09: MU prevents NSEI from submitting a Faculty Development proposal in Nuclear\nEngineering to the Nuclear Regulatory Commission, citing \u201ccosts\u201d as the reason. MU\nsimultaneously allowed the College of Engineering to submit a proposal asking for the similar\nthings from the same agency.\n2009:-MU takes away with the slot/funding for Director under \u201cCompete Missouri\u201d position\nwithout consulting the NSEI faculty. Graduate Dean maintains until August 2012 that the\nslot/funding was still in NSEI.\n2010: External Review Team visits NSEI in April. Review is highly complementary of student\nquality and satisfaction, and faculty quality, recognition and successes. It is critical of extent of\ninternal interactions and support. Review team recommends that director be appointed to work\non planning.\nNSEI faculty respond in May 2010. The administration does not follow up with them.\nOne faculty member retires, faculty request hiring, but no permission is given.\n2011: NSEI faculty win a $1.15M/4 year research award from DOE involving interactions with 3\nMU faculty from other units.\nCAMPEP accredits NSEI\u2019s Medical Physics program (both MS and PhD). MU commits in writing\ntwo new faculty positions in Medical Physics to CAMPEP), but there is no follow up.\nMarch 12, 2012: Dean of the Graduate School and Provost publicly announce that NSEI will be\nclosed on March 15, and they inform the faculty, staff and students simultaneously. The NSEI",
